/id #uuid "54330749-a8f4-51be-8aac-cb29ecd721ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305966} {:answer/id #uuid "953587b2-402b-5a0c-bce7-cd39496a001b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306556} {:answer/id #uuid "a49df960-4d11-5ec9-a9b9-c055af9e5935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306701} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0c3da5-a2db-5599-8b95-3ca3655c4ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307197}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306701} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0904c2-4875-5c9e-b479-6bfaaefefc55" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db8bd58e-f203-50b9-91f2-a939090e4f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333300" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y &={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | $0.22$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179429dc-b761-5497-8ad7-c470709381b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305369} {:answer/id #uuid "4829f5c5-43ec-54b6-af11-d8e105c268c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305853} {:answer/id #uuid "905c5151-70d4-562e-b11b-2e34ed0bf5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306520} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8abb12-c70f-5384-975f-003089e6ade9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17