ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305349} {:answer/id #uuid "298c0d44-57ed-527b-af6e-38b2be6171e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305544} {:answer/id #uuid "6b56dd7d-c137-50d9-8390-5f94c3fd8d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306179} {:answer/id #uuid "be03e8ed-8ebc-5727-b764-d5d601413e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306179} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a1ba9f-299d-5bcf-ac6d-b348f3d3c99b" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f59635f7-28c2-56d1-8760-e3dfe8a1e81a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333589" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[3,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/b0/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/64/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y &=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | $0.45$ |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | $4.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/b0/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/64/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213b19a5-0b1e-5c5e-a96f-d46ee38321c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305471} {:answer/id #uuid "485b3e62-eaa8-50f0-af29-6abfa021c9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305856} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9e056c-6075-5b47-8114-54757d2281dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306365} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c9ee29-c5f1-5