8174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086393c9-15ac-5aef-a4c1-9bd35df8569f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305232} {:answer/id #uuid "400b2b0c-e725-5c09-9d61-36aabd84fd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305775} {:answer/id #uuid "e261befa-2a8c-557b-8ddf-b71cd30ecda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307248} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ac86f9-8849-5ab6-b9c0-dc31bf5453c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307248} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18149d5-ec41-595e-a036-6b11138bd767" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42934189-fc99-5bad-a039-803cf217aa1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333602" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $0.00$ | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1367d4c3-b5b1-5690-aab2-44985cf01bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305319} {:answer/id #uuid "24668b28-99b9-5e54-a0c4-bc028c4820c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305498} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9d610b-76c5-572b-a11e-883ee62d0ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305730} {:answer/id #uuid "74516533-ce85-5ad6-abd6-3d0bfcce4ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306254} {:answer/id #uuid "c88bd5db-6ebf-5c09-b65a-02699a419dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305498} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6643fe3-e25b-594a-a4db-390d58331c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cc5509e-3cff-5470-9ead-3ffe928da922" :question/math-academy-id "q-33451" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2,0,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(6,3,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4+6+18|}{\\sqrt{1+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|28|}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6924d9b1-5a1b-5960-9047-6dace2c0f5d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306154} {:answer/id #uuid "814d39f6-0bc8-5bc2-8c52-0b58cbdd6a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306385} {:answer/id #uuid "dc83e9d5-26db-5557-a16f-a01467cfa2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307192} {:answer/id #uuid "e85efaf2-e739-544b-9816-5781974d8412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307296} {:answer/id #uuid "f08f2cc8-1389-5ef3-8d98-74db52713374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c999c1938803ff00ff445cdeb063f679e06acf47109cbcba01eeb6ed502e3d33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307192} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a67073-c266-558d-9507-3b272eced88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22d70586-a868-5b19-aa40-b97ef9910f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334620" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+1 \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/f7/f7be54fdf4b1cbe65be9730989c06b934604bc8f0b4300bd6b616ece9de7b8f5.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2t-1 \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/47/47c41f2d62aea217d56259bf9918b1eba5bed3141d2bcfcfd486241bc32df048.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c78434-5644-5944-bd2b-237260943980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb3187e3229b7fc09f530035d1dffe0e8690e8056502ecb27fcb41c274b6b218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305388} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec31079-bc7a-5f3b-88e9-d69e4a13555b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe4863ed53356be2d5a2995d72372271544f55a86846bd0179dd82cf04844f58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305443} {:answer/id #uuid "34431ba8-0a68-53bd-a0cd-45d5ba70e3a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305649} {:answer/id #uuid "495dcc98-fb5e-5f32-a772-e7718fa85ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305864} {:answer/id #uuid "e700c283-af6b-524a-bfa2-8f8e09efd37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b47e03cb4429684acceac875e9c42875af53e0fe362125d2527bb62a24da1143.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305864} :answer-field/id #uuid "27456544-da12-5383-972a-30025449f0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aba52b16-35a3-5bbb-9f22-8bbebc7d0f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-334624" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58564c40fcde276fb69732ce5926b7e350fe8963b51b21df0ce4de39e8481514.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d0/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e0/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fecab66-dba7-588d-b051-c89c33672842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93c30d-56ef-5dbb-a2c3-12f0c02b1cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305891} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0da9f4-1a35-5132-acee-bff9e95cc05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a8d9528c878089370476a45aab127a416a11595ea0b0f972708e5161f208d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306355} {:answer/id #uuid "8a151970-06b2-5c13-989f-71d57aa7a478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306471} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1b08d6-6709-5e77-8f04-4e1eb9a5d4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4c7528-85a6-56a0-a678-684c0edfdcb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2fbf3cd8-344d-52ae-9b3c-567d393e20b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334638" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t \\\\ y &=9t+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee5b833-9c64-5e6b-aab0-da9f11cd3451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19f9668a913eb5d108bf1043d23ba277035f619eca48f6d428856a5ba77b981b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305448} {:answer/id #uuid "3b15c480-edfe-542f-8483-6b2833fe4565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305713} {:answer/id #uuid "590a2be6-20b9-5f8b-abdb-aa47cd31c158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306006} {:answer/id #uuid "daf1a0fb-c311-5d2c-bdd2-fb2a1cba88cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307178} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4c08b-8d94-52a5-9594-a17424d77fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c73a9c-a0d1-5afe-aba6-629aee1682ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7a8620e-ad1d-5894-b095-0459bbd949b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-334641" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t+3 \\\\ y &=2t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4t-3 \\\\ y &=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bcdf62-51d9-5553-97e4-b8e82ddd7525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305185} {:answer/id #uuid "84cc3a62-7903-53f2-b22f-8ca83177efd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306418} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f03628-3598-5d8b-b88f-17bc239516c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306714} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc097c6-4f70-5235-9fba-6c9df3c307b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307069} {:answer/id #uuid "f636eb30-c8ec-5afe-995f-4a8cc8485d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307069} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff39c2a1-0c41-5780-9d81-0f8662b492a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8925614-d403-5814-9cbc-785e6ec5b0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-33467" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(4,15,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(6,9,2).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-5)+(-6)\\cdot 1+7\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10-6-14|}{\\sqrt{25+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-30|}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08adfa54-b220-5bee-aca3-9d8c6ff11cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305233} {:answer/id #uuid "0a672b33-5f2a-5e4e-815d-a4a23607e2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305241} {:answer/id #uuid "109123a6-0cb6-5e52-9140-4b54eec5490f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305303} {:answer/id #uuid "4587145a-7af0-5e66-be49-55684934f598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305835} {:answer/id #uuid "69421e92-e5c7-52ff-9400-b0b6e4bc6df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305835} :answer-field/id #uuid "444b855d-911a-5837-bb31-c9c182f6281d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6cde9d50-d619-5ea3-818d-eeeb232b8830" :question/math-academy-id "q-335073" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08447618-2120-543d-83ec-e215ccd38852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305228} {:answer/id #uuid "6184b1d4-ffa4-5e81-823d-f62432c74d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306092} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce2d05-ee85-52fd-8d2c-7910d844e7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306874} {:answer/id #uuid "c54f359c-411e-55e2-a259-418b96a3cdaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306989} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5e6628-28e3-5ecf-b5ce-4530143b5b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306989} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb0a724-01dd-5634-9aae-6102654ba4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff48fa6b-fda8-5ef9-88ce-a7737fd92d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-335079" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e663ec7-657b-5405-ab38-7c06c516cceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t)+5 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309221} {:answer/id #uuid "4678bd53-05f1-5959-8fb9-a11a90f9898c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309286} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8310c1-24f9-5674-839e-ccb79fd7f7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t)-1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310397} {:answer/id #uuid "e9159d47-2f62-50dd-bc0c-dd1d39d72b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310600} {:answer/id #uuid "f8633208-5163-55dd-ad12-2957a0ceeb8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310728} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdca0da8-a855-5f66-8cb7-f7ff71aac855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ec6cfc1-e844-58cb-9d89-165931802f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-335207" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454234be-d376-5533-88f7-a83f133cd37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309275} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef3094e-37eb-5b7e-bffb-9b39b48d1c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+3v(t)+4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)+4v(t)+9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309888} {:answer/id #uuid "9175fcc0-7750-5bf2-a705-6e363d2cde63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+3v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)+4v(t)-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309913} {:answer/id #uuid "9f23d3d3-6f95-5197-bea6-32d9c77e476a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310032} {:answer/id #uuid "a71774c2-bf30-5b18-8666-e551027a377c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+3v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)+4v(t)+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310032} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a55c334-725d-524b-b458-07f6f1420e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b412f2e8-8f4e-59d5-812a-2d162a6c359c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335212" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+3y(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+4y(t)+9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a6070f-c849-53e5-a30d-7451121042ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309303} {:answer/id #uuid "49ae912e-69ab-533a-9aca-d467e61f39e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309311} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfc0bb2-d8e0-582a-930e-1a7d166d8678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309439} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d019a-27d2-516c-9bba-990266da31a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309893} {:answer/id #uuid "f15addc4-0325-5db2-9004-cd4515b1d0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)+5 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)-9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309311} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ad45ec-0c42-5e94-921d-81dcd713c823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f5893f7-3112-574c-a435-c387df346ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335213" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+5y(t)-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d4842ff-27f9-5992-9863-d07fd39b2d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312330} {:answer/id #uuid "36d9c331-1296-56e1-867e-1c1f15f8f9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312417} {:answer/id #uuid "4c387986-defc-56bc-8a0b-b8a9ac845695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312591} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa0ebec-880a-5969-86bd-143812e540c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312955} {:answer/id #uuid "a53d677f-0b4d-534d-a14a-e041a7519e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312330} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea08bcfb-fe15-5af4-8217-ba4d412e8115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a30a8dd5-ba00-5881-9097-ff69e821d30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34052" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ -5\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3-1=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6572e448-2a1b-54fa-b7eb-b164a363dfcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430957} {:answer/id #uuid "77ade1d1-f580-5223-b0a2-1bf51cbff285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430959} {:answer/id #uuid "9e92c0f2-12f6-52e4-bc39-5f630e281daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430962} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b063f1-874e-516d-844c-6910584fd9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430964} {:answer/id #uuid "bd56a073-7763-5f3e-8627-e620cdb34e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430966} :answer-field/id #uuid "7741c95a-c056-57b4-9397-7a01a1ba8764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "870e5cfd-95f3-5e52-b251-41fd48145e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b21dda-de01-5604-87d6-f584e72f6811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429752} {:answer/id #uuid "682a6b44-ab7e-500b-bb7e-d04456824f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429757} {:answer/id #uuid "a7beabae-c19b-5ab1-bf80-b342b52cb235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429763} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa02ece-075b-5a23-8ec8-b922ddb2aea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429764} {:answer/id #uuid "e75b98ee-ddb1-5ceb-8b26-88ce2b2c42d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429752} :answer-field/id #uuid "484b2020-65a7-5604-96a1-0c5659cbed3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "585db78b-56e0-58b0-a767-e41e96247676" :question/math-academy-id "q-34456" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa456ff-14e6-5b4c-8bb6-5fa53d7a73e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423601} {:answer/id #uuid "1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423602} {:answer/id #uuid "2b80385e-3a37-5b43-b815-d32cf7c3607c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423603} {:answer/id #uuid "79611b73-f06c-516d-a314-79fc8a0df50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423607} {:answer/id #uuid "ce207c25-42d3-5952-b1ac-04aeb42aad0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423602} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb5ef360-4ae2-56e4-8b56-8bb54c7ef0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28419572-5f5f-5d91-aa00-334d8b1c45ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-34514" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(F).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "906c670b-ccd1-51b1-8d62-ee51e20498b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318057} {:answer/id #uuid "b00e6fdb-79a7-5f12-9565-651b74857a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318162} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa31a62-8288-5958-99a5-c106829857cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318201} {:answer/id #uuid "ced77a6e-8bf5-5d39-bac6-98d157a36706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318248} {:answer/id #uuid "d9707297-83a0-55bc-b2b9-d2dbf2352f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318057} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc094cc-b0a9-55a4-9da0-07052643faf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "deec7ef1-a437-505b-aec9-2a0d7048c769" :question/math-academy-id "q-34601" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}$ given that  $6\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ -2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-6\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{b}-6\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -48 \\\\ -58\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=24-29=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aab6359-d412-5594-9846-75e761e2adf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317696} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdefd21-573e-5f0e-84c7-9994c7e195f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317700} {:answer/id #uuid "4472fcff-17d4-5a02-a680-9de0b581a073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317788} {:answer/id #uuid "f75dceba-84c1-5c39-9174-0200a28709ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318384} {:answer/id #uuid "fde7d800-efa6-5d51-97dd-1d43ce1bba79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317700} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af871db-2403-531b-aa56-f1be63167c90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dbc23108-6d38-5169-be16-bd8ae0c5611e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34725" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d268a4-da68-5461-a5fd-4325487f2486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319793} {:answer/id #uuid "8e081505-3e87-527d-928a-050077ac043e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319815} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f2b1f8-3c4d-5787-adea-b1ec3788961d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319903} {:answer/id #uuid "dca55a5a-8830-5a03-b9ee-73e0f828dbe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320062} {:answer/id #uuid "fb52be77-be13-5cd3-9f52-a1bc052f07d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320148} :answer-field/id #uuid "e593bb99-2134-5208-8b24-97c01ed0e7f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8505e165-2993-5e50-9039-0c1c0e794caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-35292" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29de170a-c6df-528c-9251-01f3de79ef6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319525} {:answer/id #uuid "38e191bb-945f-5f74-84c7-b6dbab73d721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319566} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4489fa-8058-58fc-9d16-c24df105f95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319671} {:answer/id #uuid "da2382ba-e1ad-5327-8727-332d59ce6c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320055} {:answer/id #uuid "e11b8b77-8937-57d0-997a-adeec99d9234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319566} :answer-field/id #uuid "79cea883-b687-5151-a7fb-d722579f29c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2472ea9f-8e8e-5b80-8c7c-945a5a43c650" :question/math-academy-id "q-35297" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0077e2b0-9780-54fe-afa4-b670dc9fed69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319393} {:answer/id #uuid "3acae8da-de1f-55f4-a6ed-13701c01de6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0903cd-0495-574d-b41e-abbe3013ddf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319571} {:answer/id #uuid "87860f14-7358-57f6-a4a5-2a37b1f9c9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85f8ab-e663-568c-9644-773ac18a9dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320028}] :answe